    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d89">

                <pb facs="0175" n="145" xml:id="pg145"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    IO Files: US/A/510</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by Mr. Harley A. Notter of the United States
                            Delegation Staff of Advisers
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1947-09-29"
                            >September 29, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Hadow<anchor n="1" xml:id="d89-f1"/> told me near three o’clock today in the
                    lounge at Lake Success that he and his colleagues had noted during the day a
                    rapid development (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) against the geographic
                    principle, and (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) also against “blindly” voting
                    any longer for a set number of satellites.</p>
                <p>He mentioned as a fact that some Latin-American states were likely to vote for
                    India rather than Canada for the Security Council. The risk that Canada might
                    lose worried the British very much. The British were boosting India and Canada,
                    and they hoped we could stiffen support for Canada. Czechoslovakia might not be
                    elected to the Security Council he believed, because of the (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) factor above. The British were pressing
                    Australia for the Economic and Social Council.</p>
                <p>As to the Economic and Social Council, we spoke further at five o’clock in the
                    driveway at Lake Success. He replied to my question that Mr. Evatt had taken Mr.
                    Austin’s conversation “very well” and was “happy” to have our support on the
                    second ballot if someone or other of our slate failed to win. He said “Mr. Evatt
                    is anxious now for the news to be spread”—whereupon he leaped into his waiting
                    motor.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
